1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,066
A modern microchip contains over one
hundred billion transistors, each smaller

2
00:00:05,066 --> 00:00:08,379
than a virus, arranged in
three-dimensional layers.

3
00:00:08,379 --> 00:00:12,510
You cannot see them with an optical
microscope because they are smaller than

4
00:00:12,510 --> 00:00:14,304
the wavelengths of visible light.

5
00:00:14,304 --> 00:00:18,598
The manufacturing process that creates
them uses ultraviolet light with a

6
00:00:18,598 --> 00:00:22,775
wavelength of thirteen point five
nanometers — forty times shorter than

7
00:00:22,775 --> 00:00:27,599
violet — and every structure is built by
repeating the same four steps hundreds of

8
00:00:27,599 --> 00:00:27,952
times.

9
00:00:27,952 --> 00:00:30,638
The starting material is a silicon wafer.

10
00:00:30,638 --> 00:00:35,224
Silicon is element fourteen, a
semiconductor — it conducts electricity

11
00:00:35,224 --> 00:00:38,827
only when you add specific impurities or
apply voltage.

12
00:00:38,827 --> 00:00:42,373
The wafer is not cut from a natural
crystal. It is grown.

13
00:00:42,373 --> 00:00:47,575
A seed crystal of silicon touches molten
silicon in a crucible, and as the seed is

14
00:00:47,575 --> 00:00:52,459
slowly pulled upward and rotated, atoms
lock into the seed's lattice pattern.

15
00:00:52,459 --> 00:00:57,652
The result is a cylindrical ingot of
single-crystal silicon, meaning the atomic

16
00:00:57,652 --> 00:01:02,056
arrangement is continuous and unbroken
across the entire structure.

17
00:01:02,056 --> 00:01:06,957
The Czochralski process, developed in
nineteen fifty, produces ingots up to two

18
00:01:06,957 --> 00:01:10,307
meters long and three hundred millimeters
in diameter.

19
00:01:10,307 --> 00:01:15,065
The ingot is sliced into wafers less than
one millimeter thick, polished until the

20
00:01:15,065 --> 00:01:19,533
surface roughness is under one nanometer,
and cleaned to remove particles and

21
00:01:19,533 --> 00:01:20,520
organic residues.

22
00:01:20,520 --> 00:01:26,404
SEMI International Standards specify the
crystallographic orientation, dopant

23
00:01:26,404 --> 00:01:29,155
concentration, and surface flatness.

24
00:01:29,155 --> 00:01:34,376
The wafer is the foundation — every
transistor will be built on this surface.

25
00:01:34,376 --> 00:01:37,382
Photolithography is the core patterning
method.

26
00:01:37,382 --> 00:01:42,244
A liquid polymer called photoresist is
spin-coated onto the wafer, forming a

27
00:01:42,244 --> 00:01:45,059
uniform film a few hundred nanometers
thick.

28
00:01:45,059 --> 00:01:48,332
The wafer is soft-baked to evaporate
solvents.

29
00:01:48,332 --> 00:01:53,954
A photomask — a quartz plate with opaque
chrome patterns — is aligned above the

30
00:01:53,954 --> 00:01:57,725
wafer, and ultraviolet light shines
through the mask.

31
00:01:57,725 --> 00:02:02,744
Where light hits the resist, chemical
bonds break or form, depending on whether

32
00:02:02,744 --> 00:02:05,285
the resist is positive or negative tone.

33
00:02:05,285 --> 00:02:10,507
The wafer is developed in a solvent that
removes either the exposed or unexposed

34
00:02:10,507 --> 00:02:13,248
resist, leaving a patterned polymer layer.

35
00:02:13,248 --> 00:02:14,998
This pattern is a stencil.

36
00:02:14,998 --> 00:02:20,449
Material underneath the open areas can now
be etched away, or new material can be

37
00:02:20,449 --> 00:02:22,267
deposited into those areas.

38
00:02:22,267 --> 00:02:27,021
After etching or deposition, the remaining
resist is stripped with plasma or

39
00:02:27,021 --> 00:02:27,584
solvents.

40
00:02:27,584 --> 00:02:28,719
The cycle repeats.

41
00:02:28,719 --> 00:02:33,892
A modern processor requires three hundred
to five hundred photolithography cycles,

42
00:02:33,892 --> 00:02:37,424
each adding or removing material in a
different pattern.

43
00:02:37,424 --> 00:02:42,915
ASMINTERNATIONAL and IEEE publish the
process parameters — exposure dose, focus

44
00:02:42,915 --> 00:02:48,544
depth, overlay accuracy — that determine
whether the pattern transfers correctly.

45
00:02:48,544 --> 00:02:52,760
The smallest feature you can print with
light is roughly proportional to the

46
00:02:52,760 --> 00:02:53,371
wavelength.

47
00:02:53,371 --> 00:02:59,073
Visible light ranges from four hundred
nanometers for violet to seven hundred

48
00:02:59,073 --> 00:03:00,480
nanometers for red.

49
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:05,110
Early photolithography used mercury lamps
emitting at three hundred sixty-five

50
00:03:05,110 --> 00:03:09,147
nanometers and could pattern features a
few hundred nanometers wide.

51
00:03:09,147 --> 00:03:13,954
As transistors shrank, the industry moved
to deep ultraviolet at one hundred

52
00:03:13,954 --> 00:03:16,864
ninety-three nanometers, using excimer
lasers.

53
00:03:16,864 --> 00:03:21,953
Current leading-edge chips use extreme
ultraviolet lithography at thirteen point

54
00:03:21,953 --> 00:03:22,971
five nanometers.

55
00:03:22,971 --> 00:03:28,345
EUV light is generated by firing a
high-power laser at molten tin droplets,

56
00:03:28,345 --> 00:03:32,000
vaporizing them into plasma that emits EUV
photons.

57
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:37,225
The light is so strongly absorbed by air
and most materials that the entire optical

58
00:03:37,225 --> 00:03:42,324
path must be in vacuum, and the mirrors
are multilayer coatings of molybdenum and

59
00:03:42,324 --> 00:03:46,731
silicon, each layer a few nanometers
thick, designed to reflect EUV by

60
00:03:46,731 --> 00:03:48,368
constructive interference.

61
00:03:48,368 --> 00:03:53,941
ASML in the Netherlands is the only
manufacturer of EUV lithography systems.

62
00:03:53,941 --> 00:03:57,463
Each machine costs over one hundred fifty
million dollars.

63
00:03:57,463 --> 00:04:02,503
The wavelength is measured by spectrometry
and published by NIST and SEMI standards

64
00:04:02,503 --> 00:04:02,928
bodies.

65
00:04:02,928 --> 00:04:07,761
After the resist is patterned, the exposed
material must be removed.

66
00:04:07,761 --> 00:04:13,021
Wet etching uses liquid chemicals —
hydrofluoric acid for silicon dioxide,

67
00:04:13,021 --> 00:04:15,083
phosphoric acid for aluminum.

68
00:04:15,083 --> 00:04:20,425
The etch is isotropic, meaning it removes
material equally in all directions, which

69
00:04:20,425 --> 00:04:23,643
limits how small and vertical the features
can be.

70
00:04:23,643 --> 00:04:28,544
Dry etching uses plasma — ionized gas — in
a vacuum chamber.

71
00:04:28,544 --> 00:04:33,343
Reactive ions are accelerated toward the
wafer, and they chemically react with and

72
00:04:33,343 --> 00:04:35,976
physically sputter away the exposed
material.

73
00:04:35,976 --> 00:04:40,580
Plasma etching can be anisotropic,
removing material primarily in the

74
00:04:40,580 --> 00:04:44,184
vertical direction, which allows deep
narrow trenches.

75
00:04:44,184 --> 00:04:48,817
The etch rate, selectivity between
materials, and sidewall angle are

76
00:04:48,817 --> 00:04:54,131
controlled by gas chemistry, pressure,
radio frequency power, and temperature.

77
00:04:54,131 --> 00:04:59,343
NIST and Fraunhofer Institute publish etch
rate data for silicon, silicon dioxide,

78
00:04:59,343 --> 00:05:02,776
and silicon nitride under different plasma
conditions.

79
00:05:02,776 --> 00:05:07,930
The etch must stop on an underlying layer
without removing it — this is called etch

80
00:05:07,930 --> 00:05:12,649
selectivity, and it is verified by
cross-sectional electron microscopy after

81
00:05:12,649 --> 00:05:13,395
the process.

82
00:05:13,395 --> 00:05:16,745
Between etch steps, new materials are
deposited.

83
00:05:16,745 --> 00:05:22,398
Chemical vapor deposition flows reactive
gases over a heated wafer, and the gases

84
00:05:22,398 --> 00:05:26,307
decompose or react on the surface, leaving
a solid film.

85
00:05:26,307 --> 00:05:29,580
Silane gas decomposes to deposit silicon.

86
00:05:29,580 --> 00:05:33,651
Oxygen and silane together deposit silicon
dioxide.

87
00:05:33,651 --> 00:05:37,144
Tungsten hexafluoride and hydrogen deposit
tungsten metal.

88
00:05:37,144 --> 00:05:41,179
Physical vapor deposition uses a
sputtering target — a block of the

89
00:05:41,179 --> 00:05:44,251
material you want to deposit — in a vacuum
chamber.

90
00:05:44,251 --> 00:05:48,897
Argon ions bombard the target, knocking
atoms off, and those atoms travel across

91
00:05:48,897 --> 00:05:51,104
the chamber and condense on the wafer.

92
00:05:51,104 --> 00:05:55,244
Copper interconnects are deposited by
sputtering a copper seed layer, then

93
00:05:55,244 --> 00:05:56,923
electroplating thicker copper.

94
00:05:56,923 --> 00:06:01,942
Film thickness is measured by
ellipsometry, which analyzes how polarized

95
00:06:01,942 --> 00:06:03,893
light reflects off the film.

96
00:06:03,893 --> 00:06:08,210
Uniformity across the wafer must be within
two percent.

97
00:06:08,210 --> 00:06:14,331
SEMI standards specify thickness,
composition, and stress for deposited

98
00:06:14,331 --> 00:06:19,449
The film's crystal structure and impurity
levels are measured by X-ray diffraction

99
00:06:19,449 --> 00:06:21,696
and secondary ion mass spectrometry.

100
00:06:21,696 --> 00:06:23,834
Pure silicon is a poor conductor.

101
00:06:23,834 --> 00:06:28,499
Doping adds atoms of other elements to
change its electrical properties.

102
00:06:28,499 --> 00:06:33,712
Boron, with three valence electrons,
creates positive charge carriers called

103
00:06:33,712 --> 00:06:38,423
Phosphorus or arsenic, with five valence
electrons, creates negative charge

104
00:06:38,423 --> 00:06:40,056
carriers called electrons.

105
00:06:40,056 --> 00:06:45,681
Doping is done by ion implantation — ions
are accelerated to high energy and shot

106
00:06:45,681 --> 00:06:49,779
into the wafer, embedding themselves at a
controlled depth.

107
00:06:49,779 --> 00:06:53,765
The dose and energy determine the
concentration and depth profile.

108
00:06:53,765 --> 00:06:58,537
After implantation, the wafer is annealed
at high temperature to repair crystal

109
00:06:58,537 --> 00:07:03,007
damage and move the dopant atoms into
electrically active positions in the

110
00:07:03,007 --> 00:07:03,491
lattice.

111
00:07:03,491 --> 00:07:07,855
Doping concentrations range from one times
ten to the fifteenth atoms per cubic

112
00:07:07,855 --> 00:07:12,385
centimeter for lightly doped regions to
one times ten to the twentieth for heavily

113
00:07:12,385 --> 00:07:13,213
doped contacts.

114
00:07:13,213 --> 00:07:17,325
These concentrations are measured by
secondary ion mass spectrometry and

115
00:07:17,325 --> 00:07:19,096
spreading resistance profiling.

116
00:07:19,096 --> 00:07:23,736
NIST provides reference materials with
certified dopant concentrations.

117
00:07:23,736 --> 00:07:28,834
The junction depth and abruptness are
measured by cross-sectional transmission

118
00:07:28,834 --> 00:07:30,141
electron microscopy.

119
00:07:30,141 --> 00:07:35,360
I went looking for the actual yield rate —
what fraction of chips on a wafer work —

120
00:07:35,360 --> 00:07:37,560
and manufacturers don't publish it.

121
00:07:37,560 --> 00:07:42,356
I could describe the process in exact
steps and still not answer that adjacent

122
00:07:42,356 --> 00:07:42,909
question.

123
00:07:42,909 --> 00:07:47,730
After depositing and etching multiple
layers, the wafer surface becomes rough

124
00:07:47,730 --> 00:07:48,419
and uneven.

125
00:07:48,419 --> 00:07:51,209
Chemical mechanical polishing flattens it.

126
00:07:51,209 --> 00:07:56,657
The wafer is pressed face-down against a
rotating pad while a slurry flows between

127
00:07:56,657 --> 00:07:56,989
them.

128
00:07:56,989 --> 00:08:02,252
The slurry contains abrasive particles —
silica or ceria — and chemicals that react

129
00:08:02,252 --> 00:08:04,344
with the material being polished.

130
00:08:04,344 --> 00:08:09,685
The combination of mechanical abrasion and
chemical reaction removes material faster

131
00:08:09,685 --> 00:08:13,181
from high spots than low spots, flattening
the surface.

132
00:08:13,181 --> 00:08:17,957
Copper CMP uses an acidic slurry with
hydrogen peroxide.

133
00:08:17,957 --> 00:08:20,515
Oxide CMP uses a basic slurry.

134
00:08:20,515 --> 00:08:25,747
The polishing rate, selectivity, and
surface finish depend on slurry chemistry,

135
00:08:25,747 --> 00:08:28,595
pad material, pressure, and rotation
speed.

136
00:08:28,595 --> 00:08:33,942
The process is monitored by measuring the
wafer thickness and surface roughness with

137
00:08:33,942 --> 00:08:35,469
atomic force microscopy.

138
00:08:35,469 --> 00:08:39,904
SEMI standards specify the post-CMP
flatness and defect density.

139
00:08:39,904 --> 00:08:44,339
Without CMP, photolithography could not
focus on the next layer.

140
00:08:44,339 --> 00:08:48,701
The evidence for how microchips are made
comes from the process specifications

141
00:08:48,701 --> 00:08:52,839
published by standards bodies, the
metrology data collected at every step,

142
00:08:52,839 --> 00:08:55,747
and the cross-sectional imaging of
finished devices.

143
00:08:55,747 --> 00:09:01,414
NIST provides measurement standards for
wavelengths, film thickness, and dopant

144
00:09:01,414 --> 00:09:02,418
concentration.

145
00:09:02,418 --> 00:09:08,158
SEMI publishes specifications for wafer
dimensions, film properties, and process

146
00:09:08,158 --> 00:09:08,947
tolerances.

147
00:09:08,947 --> 00:09:14,466
IEEE and ASMINTERNATIONAL document
lithography parameters and material

148
00:09:14,466 --> 00:09:20,262
Every fabrication facility measures every
wafer at dozens of points in the process —

149
00:09:20,262 --> 00:09:25,574
film thickness, etch depth, dopant
profile, defect density — and the data are

150
00:09:25,574 --> 00:09:27,437
compared to specifications.

151
00:09:27,437 --> 00:09:32,189
Transmission electron microscopy images
the final transistor structure at atomic

152
00:09:32,189 --> 00:09:37,000
resolution, confirming that the layers are
where the process intended them to be.

153
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:41,909
The process is known in detail, but the
yield — the fraction of chips on a wafer

154
00:09:41,909 --> 00:09:46,267
that function correctly — varies and is
not published by manufacturers.

155
00:09:46,267 --> 00:09:50,993
Estimates range from seventy to
ninety-five percent for mature processes,

156
00:09:50,993 --> 00:09:52,288
lower for new nodes.

157
00:09:52,288 --> 00:09:55,145
A single defect can disable a chip.

158
00:09:55,145 --> 00:10:01,595
Particles, contamination, misalignment,
and process variation all reduce yield.

159
00:10:01,595 --> 00:10:06,332
The relationship between defect density
and yield is modeled statistically, but

160
00:10:06,332 --> 00:10:09,451
the actual yield for a given product is
proprietary.

161
00:10:09,451 --> 00:10:13,851
The evidence tells us how the process
works and how it is measured, not how

162
00:10:13,851 --> 00:10:15,728
often it succeeds in production.

163
00:10:15,728 --> 00:10:21,066
Microchips are made by repeating four
steps — deposit, pattern, etch, measure —

164
00:10:21,066 --> 00:10:26,539
hundreds of times, each cycle adding or
removing material in a different pattern.

165
00:10:26,539 --> 00:10:31,451
The evidence is the published process
parameters, the metrology data, and the

166
00:10:31,451 --> 00:10:34,832
electron microscope images of the finished
structure.
